From 27cb7db997d87ad0f17891e13e9c299af8c5a4af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wray-lee <wray-lee@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 02:22:42 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 13/15] fix(telegram): keep the live draft owned by the running
 turn

The per-chat live-draft slot (draft_buffers) had no notion of turn
ownership. Every inbound message called startTypingTurn -> beginDraftTurn
-> beginDraftStateLocked, which cleared the chat's draft buffer and
rotated its draft_id, and the deferred finishDraftTurn then deleted the
slot outright. When a second message (or an interactive callback) was
handled while a turn was still generating, it reset/deleted the draft
that the running turn's StreamCtx still held, so every later streamed
reasoning chunk hit the  guard in
sendDraftChunkForTurn and was silently dropped. The user was left with
only the "Interim result is still being prepared." heartbeat.

Make the draft owned by the turn that began it:

- DraftState gains an  flag, set when a turn claims the draft.
- beginDraftTurn refuses to clobber an active draft (returns 0), so a
  second caller starts no draft and cannot orphan the running one.
- startTypingTurn claims the draft before touching the typing loop and
  returns a TypingTurn{started, draft_id}: when it cannot claim a draft
  it leaves the owner (and its typing indicator) untouched.
- startTyping, the generic Channel entry point the daemon/webhook inbound
  indicator uses, now starts only the typing loop: it must not claim a
  draft, because that path releases the draft through sendEvent rather
  than through finishDraftTurn.
- processTelegramMessage and handleTelegramInteractiveCallback consult
  the inbound router before starting the typing/draft turn, so an
  injected/dropped message never resets the running turn's draft.

The daemon path creates the draft lazily through sendEvent(.chunk) and
releases it on every turn-final delivery, including the inline-choices
(sendRich) and replace_message (editMessage) paths that bypass
sendEvent(.final), so the next turn starts from a clean, empty draft.

finishDraftTurn already clears only its own draft id, and a queued
message that later runs its own turn gets a fresh draft because the
previous turn's slot was already removed.
